{"questions":[{"question_id":"1lg0w154t","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2007 Paper 1 Offline","year":2007,"examDate":2007,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","topic":"bond-parameters","type":"mcq","marks":3,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"pstatement-1--boron-always-forms-covalent-bondp-pbe-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-x8awrplpg9zklipb","content":"<p><b>Statement 1 :</b> Boron always forms covalent bond.</p>\n<p><b>Statement 2 :</b> The small size of B<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.232ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.2 986.7 833.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(33,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> favours formation of covalent bond.</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is NOT a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is False"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Statement 1 is False, Statement 2 is True"}],"correct_options":["A"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Statement 1:</strong> <em>Boron always forms covalent bonds.</em>  </p>\n<p>→ Boron has a small size and high ionization energy. It does not easily lose electrons to form <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.95ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.2 1745.7 833.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(792,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ions, since removing three electrons requires a lot of energy.  </p>\n<p>→ Therefore, boron compounds (like BF₃, BCl₃, BH₃, etc.) are <strong>covalent</strong> in nature.  </p>\n<p>✅ <strong>Statement 1 is True.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Statement 2:</strong> <em>The small size of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.95ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.2 1745.7 833.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(792,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> favors formation of covalent bond.</em>  </p>\n<p>→ According to Fajan’s rules, small cations with high charge density have strong polarizing power, which distorts the electron cloud of the anion, leading to covalency.  </p>\n<p>→ <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.95ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.2 1745.7 833.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(792,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is extremely small and highly charged, therefore its compounds are predominantly covalent.  </p>\n<p>✅ <strong>Statement 2 is True.</strong></p>\n<p>Furthermore, <strong>Statement 2 gives the correct reason</strong> why boron compounds are covalent — due to the small size and high charge of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.95ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.2 1745.7 833.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(792,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.</p>\n<p>✅ <strong>Correct Option: A</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Answer: Option A — Both statements are true, and Statement 2 is the correct explanation for Statement 1.</strong></p>"}]}